---
// Private case study: FujiFilm SonoSite iViz (2013). Password-gated (lib/gate.ts), noindex.
// Same substance as the davidpuerto.com study, re-skinned in the Unakin system: dark glass,
// Neotrax display headings, whiteboard sketches inked in light on dark, mint for the thumb.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { isUnlocked, gateConfigured } from '../../lib/gate';
export const prerender = false;

const unlocked = isUnlocked(Astro.cookies);
const wrong = Astro.url.searchParams.get('e') === '1';
const configured = gateConfigured();
const here = Astro.url.pathname + (Astro.url.search.replace(/[?&]e=1/, '') || ''); // keep ?deck=1 through the unlock
// ?deck=1 turns the page into the portfolio-review deck: every section is a slide, plus the
// run-of-show slides (cold open, frame, living portfolio, segue, AI-today, throughline, Q&A).
const deck = Astro.url.searchParams.get('deck') === '1';
---
<Base title="SonoSite iViz — a case study · Unakin" description="Private case study." path={here} noindex={true}>
  {!unlocked ? (
    <main class="lock">
      <section class="lock-card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Private case study</p>
        <h1 class="display-h lock-h"><span class="stroke">SonoSite</span><br /><span class="grad-text glint">iViz.</span></h1>
        <p class="muted lock-sub">Hardware-device UX · one-thumb radial navigation · prototyped in code, 2013. Shared with a password for portfolio review.</p>
        <form method="POST" action="/api/unlock" class="lock-form">
          <input type="hidden" name="next" value={here} />
          <script is:inline>
            // A deep link (#b7) never reaches the server, so it would be lost through the unlock
            // redirect — carry the hash along in `next` so you land on the beat you were sent to.
            (function () { var f = document.currentScript.closest('form'), n = f && f.querySelector('[name=next]'); if (n && location.hash) n.value += location.hash; })();
          </script>
          <label>Password<input name="password" type="password" required autocomplete="current-password" autofocus placeholder="••••••••" /></label>
          <button class="btn" type="submit">Open the study →</button>
        </form>
        {wrong && <p class="lock-err">That's not it — check the note that came with the link.</p>}
        {!configured && <p class="lock-err">The gate isn't configured on this server yet.</p>}
        <p class="muted lock-foot">Need the password? <a href="mailto:hello@unakin.com?subject=SonoSite%20case%20study">hello@unakin.com</a></p>
      </section>
    </main>
  ) : (
    <main class={deck ? 'cs deck' : 'cs'} data-deck={deck ? '1' : undefined}>
      {deck && (<>
        <!-- ============ DECK: COLD OPEN ============ -->
        <section class="block container deck-only slide-title" data-notes="Screen-share the portrait hero first (davidpuerto.com) and drive it with gaze + voice. Say: 'Everything I'm showing you, I'm steering with my eyes and my voice. This is my portfolio — but it's also the argument: I build interfaces that meet every person through whatever input they have.' Fallback: pointer + voice, demo gaze once, controlled.">
          <p class="eyebrow">Portfolio review · Microsoft Windows &amp; Devices · Senior Product Designer</p>
          <h1 class="display-h cs-title"><span class="stroke">David</span><br /><span class="grad-text glint">Puerto.</span></h1>
          <p class="lede">For twelve years I've designed novel ways for people to interact with computers — and used AI to build things people weren't sure were possible, then made them trustworthy enough to ship and adopt.</p>
          <p class="hint mono">→ arrows to advance · N notes · F fullscreen</p>
        </section>

        <!-- ============ DECK: FRAME ============ -->
        <section class="block container deck-only" data-notes="Name the three threads. Tell them the shape: the interaction system, then the systems-and-trust work at Apple, then deploy something to production live.">
          <p class="eyebrow">The frame</p>
          <h2 class="display-h hollow sec-h">Three threads, one hour.</h2>
          <div class="threads">
            <div class="stat"><b>01</b><span>Novel multimodal interaction</span></div>
            <div class="stat"><b>02</b><span>AI as a force multiplier</span></div>
            <div class="stat"><b>03</b><span>Earning trust · driving adoption</span></div>
          </div>
          <p class="lede">The interaction system → the systems-and-trust work at Apple → ship something to production, live → the twelve-year throughline.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- ============ DECK: ACT 1 — LIVING PORTFOLIO ============ -->
        <section class="block container deck-only" data-notes="Portrait wall: brush / scatter / return across every input. The HUD cursor: radial HUD, bloom-open previews, pointer / keyboard / gaze (head+eyes, blink-to-click) / voice. Lead with the OUTCOME for Dame + Heinzen: 'someone who can't use a mouse operates this fully by gaze, voice, or switch. Every path is complete on its own; nothing's gated behind one modality; on-device. Disability is the opportunity.' Then, briefly, receipts: S360 + bug bashes with the teams who live these barriers, shipping fixes iteratively to WCAG 2.1 AA. Systems note (Sharma/Pendlay): one input bus — every modality is a small adapter over a shared core; adding voice or gaze changed the core by zero lines.">
          <p class="eyebrow">Act 1 · The living portfolio</p>
          <h2 class="display-h hollow sec-h">Multimodal. Inclusive by architecture.</h2>
          <div class="threads">
            <div class="stat"><b>Gaze</b><span>head + eyes · blink to click</span></div>
            <div class="stat"><b>Voice</b><span>"scatter" · "come back"</span></div>
            <div class="stat"><b>Hand</b><span>point · pinch · fist to scroll</span></div>
            <div class="stat"><b>1 bus</b><span>every modality is an adapter over one core</span></div>
          </div>
          <p class="lede">Start with who gains: someone who can't use a mouse — a motor disability, an injury, or just hands full — operates this fully by gaze, voice, or switch. Every path is complete on its own. Nothing is gated behind one modality. It's on-device.</p>
          <p class="hint mono">→ live: davidpuerto.com · the HUD cursor</p>
        </section>

        <!-- ============ DECK: ACT 2 TITLE ============ -->
        <section class="block container deck-only slide-title" data-notes="Elevate iViz: research-grounded, hardware, gesture, A/B-tested — tailor-made for Kait (hardware + iteration) and Sarah (research in the loop). Show the messy middle.">
          <p class="eyebrow">Act 1 · the inciting incident</p>
          <h2 class="display-h cs-title"><span class="stroke">SonoSite</span><br /><span class="grad-text glint">iViz, 2013.</span></h2>
          <p class="lede">A radial thumb gesture on a handheld ultrasound — prototyped in code, validated in two phases, shipped. The thumb-wheel that became the cursor.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- ============ DECK: THE PROBLEM ============ -->
        <section class="block container deck-only slide-title" data-notes="Name the problem in one line and let it sit: 'The body sets the rules. Not the screen.' Everything in the next twelve minutes is that sentence, tested.">
          <p class="eyebrow">Act 1 · the problem</p>
          <h2 class="display-h cs-title"><span class="stroke">The body sets</span><br /><span class="grad-text glint">the rules.</span></h2>
          <p class="lede">Not the screen. A medic has one hand on the probe — so the whole interface belongs to a thumb. Design the input to fit how the body actually moves, and everything else follows.</p>
        </section>
      </>)}

      <!-- ============ HERO ============ -->
      <section class="cs-hero" data-notes="Set the object in their hands. &quot;This is iViz — a handheld ultrasound the size of a phone. Star Trek tricorder, for real. It shipped in 2015; I prototyped its interaction in 2013.&quot; Then the frame for THIS room: &quot;For a Windows & Devices team, this is my origin story for input: a novel approach to touch — the thumb's own radius as the vehicle — so clinicians and special-operations medics could shed pack weight and still deliver life-saving care to critical patients. The body sets the rules, not the screen.&quot; Lands for: Kait (hardware), Sarah (real users).">
        <!-- full-bleed scan UI behind the title; parallax + slow settle via GSAP -->
        <div class="hero-bg" aria-hidden="true">
          <img src="/images/case-studies/sonosite/sonosite2.jpg" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" />
          <div class="hero-scrim"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="container hero-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow" data-reveal>Case study · FujiFilm SonoSite · 2013 → shipped 2015</p>
          <h1 class="display-h cs-title" data-reveal>
            <span class="stroke">iViz — think</span><br />
            <span class="grad-text glint">Tricorder.</span>
          </h1>
          <ul class="keypoints" data-reveal>
            <li>Hardware-device UX</li>
            <li>One-thumb radial navigation</li>
            <li>Prototyped in code, on the device's footprint</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="scroll-cue mono" data-reveal>↓ scroll</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ HOW I GOT HERE (the candid lead-in) ============ -->
      <section class="block container narrow leadin" data-notes="Slow down here — this is the human beat. &quot;Twenty years ago I was building real software in Flash, and every year the ceiling on what I could do in JavaScript got higher. Thirteen years ago I moved to Seattle to take the craft further — and found myself on an industrial design team, making a physical product for human hands for the first time. I didn't know if I could do it. I thought I could. The Experience Team of industrial designers let me drive.&quot; Don't rush the last line.">
        <p class="eyebrow" data-reveal>How I got here</p>
        <div class="lines">
          <h3 data-reveal><em>Twenty years ago I was building software in Flash.</em><span>Real applications — and every year the ceiling on what I could do in JavaScript got higher. I kept leveling up toward it.</span></h3>
          <h3 data-reveal><em>Thirteen years ago I moved to Seattle to take the craft further.</em><span>And found myself on an industrial design team, making a physical product for human hands for the first time in my life.</span></h3>
          <h3 data-reveal><em>I didn't know if I could do it.</em><span>I thought I could. I wanted to take a stab at it. The Experience Team of industrial designers let me drive — and that was the opportunity of a lifetime.</span></h3>
          <h3 data-reveal><em>One hand on the probe. One thumb on the device.</em><span>That's the whole brief.</span></h3>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ ROLE ============ -->
      <section class="block container" data-notes="One glance, keep moving. &quot;Industrial design, interaction design, human factors, mech eng, one software engineer. The industrial designers shaped the object — they didn't code. I was brought in to make the gesture real: the bridge between the shell and the boards. HTML/CSS/JS on an iPad Mini, because it matched the device's footprint — every test true-to-hand and true-to-scale.&quot;">
        <div class="breakdown">
          <div data-reveal>
            <h6>Project</h6>
            <p>FujiFilm SonoSite <strong>iViz</strong> — a handheld, pocket-class ultrasound for military and medical professionals. Star Trek Tricorder, for real.</p>
            <h6>Team</h6>
            <p>The "Experience Team" inside the Brand Studio: industrial design, interaction design, human factors, mechanical engineering, one software engineer. The industrial designers shaped the <em>object</em>; they didn't code. I was brought in to make the gesture real — the bridge between the shell and the boards.</p>
          </div>
          <div data-reveal>
            <h6>My role</h6>
            <ul>
              <li>UX + UI prototyping (HTML5 / CSS3 / JS)</li>
              <li>Interaction &amp; gesture design</li>
              <li>The design-engineering bridge between ID and engineering</li>
              <li>User research: A/B, surveys, direct observation</li>
            </ul>
            <h6>Tools</h6>
            <ul>
              <li>HTML5 / CSS3 / JavaScript</li>
              <li>iPad Mini — the target device's footprint, so every test was true-to-hand</li>
              <li>Whiteboard first, then code</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div data-reveal>
            <h6>Timeline</h6>
            <ul class="timeline">
              <li>2013 — exploration + touch prototypes</li>
              <li>2013 — two-phase validation</li>
              <li>2015 — iViz ships</li>
            </ul>
            <h6>The brief</h6>
            <p>A diagnostic imaging device you carry like a phone, operated by people whose other hand is always full. Intuitive, one-handed, safe under pressure.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ USERS ============ -->
      <section class="block container narrow" data-notes="The constraint meets the people. &quot;This wasn't a consumer app. Nurses, sonographers, 18D Special Forces medics, 68W combat medics, EMTs in the back of a moving rig. They were shedding pack weight and carrying diagnostic imaging into places where you don't get to set the device down, look away, or regrip — because the other hand is holding the probe against a patient who may be dying. So the whole interface belongs to a thumb.&quot;  Then the triage reality, plainly: &quot;And it is rarely one patient. Gunshot wounds, an IED, multiple casualties — the medic has to decide who is most critical and who is most likely to be saved, and render aid in that order. An image that takes two hands and a menu arrives too late for someone.&quot; Say it once, quietly. Don't dramatize. Then the principle, and this is the Surface Adaptive Kit line for Sarah: &quot;Meet people where they are. That's how I define the design constraints, understand the human personas, and truly empathize with what they're trying to accomplish.&quot; Sarah + Dave: real users, real stakes, from day one.">
        <span class="ghost-word" aria-hidden="true">HANDS</span>
        <p class="eyebrow">The constraint meets the people</p>
        <h2 class="display-h hollow sec-h">Their other hand is on the patient.</h2>
        <p class="lede" data-reveal>This wasn't a consumer app. The people holding this device were shedding pack weight and carrying diagnostic imaging into places where you don't get to set it down, look away, or regrip. Every persona shares one fact: <strong>one hand is on the transducer, so the whole interface belongs to a thumb.</strong></p>
        <p class="lede">And the field is rarely one patient. Gunshot wounds, an IED, multiple casualties — a medic has to decide who is most critical and who is most likely to be saved, and render aid in that order. A diagnostic image that takes two hands and a menu is an image that arrives too late for someone.</p>
        <p class="lede"><strong>Meet people where they are.</strong> That's how I define the design constraints, understand the human personas, and truly empathize with what they're trying to accomplish.</p>
        <div class="personas">
          {[
            ['Hospital nurse', 'the bedside triage', ['Bedside, standing, often gloved, moving between patients.', 'Probe on the patient; device in the other hand.', "Can't afford a second hand to reach a menu, or a slip that discards a capture."]],
            ['Sonographer', 'lives in depth and gain', ['Adjusts depth and gain constantly while watching the image, not the controls.', 'Probe angled precisely; device thumb-driven without looking.', "Can't afford overshooting a tissue layer, or hunting for a slider."]],
            ['18D', 'Special Forces medical sergeant', ['Austere, unpredictable, weight-critical. Every gram and every second counts.', 'Probe on a casualty; device gripped one-handed, maybe in the dark.', "Can't afford two hands, fine motor precision, or nested menus."]],
            ['68W', 'Army combat medic', ['Point-of-injury care under stress; gloved; equipment must just work.', 'Probe steady; the thumb does everything else.', "Can't afford a control that needs a grip change, or a gesture that isn't forgiving."]],
            ['EMT', 'first responders', ['Roadside, in the back of a moving rig, at a scene that is still unfolding — gloved, braced, on the clock.', 'Probe on the patient; device in the hand that is also steadying them.', "Can't afford to look down, dig through a menu, or lose a capture to a bump in the road."]],
          ].map(([name, tag, facts]) => (
            <article class="persona" data-reveal>
              <h4>{name} <span class="muted">— {tag}</span></h4>
              <ul>{(facts as string[]).map((f) => <li>{f}</li>)}</ul>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ SKETCH 1 ============ -->
      <section class="block container narrow" data-notes="&quot;In ordinary mobile design the hand holding the phone has a free partner. Here it doesn't. One hand holds the device, stabilizes it, navigates, changes depth, adjusts gain, freezes and saves. One-handed use wasn't a nice-to-have — it was the physics of the task, and it decided everything downstream.&quot; Point at the arc on the sketch: &quot;a thumb moves on an arc, not a grid.&quot;">
        <h2 class="display-h hollow sec-h" data-reveal>One hand is always full.</h2>
        <p class="lede" data-reveal>In ordinary mobile design, the hand holding the phone has a free partner. In point-of-care ultrasound it doesn't: the dominant hand is holding the transducer against the patient. That leaves exactly one hand to hold the device, stabilize it, navigate, change depth, adjust gain, freeze and save. One-handed use wasn't a nice-to-have — it was the physics of the task, and it decided everything downstream.</p>
        <figure class="sketch" data-reveal>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 900 420" role="img" aria-label="Hand sketch: a medic with the transducer on the patient in one hand and the device in the other, the thumb's arc drawn on the device.">
            <defs>
              <filter id="sketch" x="-5%" y="-5%" width="110%" height="110%">
                <feTurbulence type="fractalNoise" baseFrequency="0.02" numOctaves="2" seed="7" result="n"/>
                <feDisplacementMap in="SourceGraphic" in2="n" scale="3.2" xChannelSelector="R" yChannelSelector="G"/>
              </filter>
            </defs>
            <g filter="url(#sketch)">
              <path class="ink" d="M40 300 C 120 250, 260 250, 360 300 S 560 350, 640 300"/>
              <path class="ink-thin" d="M60 330 C 160 290, 300 290, 380 330"/>
              <text class="hand small" x="70" y="365">patient</text>
              <path class="ink" d="M250 200 l -10 60 l 20 25 l 30 -8 l 12 -50"/>
              <path class="ink-thin" d="M262 198 l -6 52 M276 200 l 2 50 M290 203 l 6 47"/>
              <rect class="ink" x="262" y="268" width="46" height="26" rx="6" transform="rotate(-8 285 281)"/>
              <text class="hand" x="180" y="185">transducer / probe</text>
              <path class="ink-thin" d="M275 190 q 25 -20 40 -5"/>
              <rect class="ink" x="560" y="120" width="150" height="230" rx="18"/>
              <rect class="ink-thin" x="574" y="140" width="122" height="130" rx="6"/>
              <path class="ink" d="M700 300 c 40 10, 60 40, 40 80 c -30 30, -90 20, -110 -20"/>
              <path class="ink-thin" d="M715 310 l 20 -10 M725 335 l 24 -6"/>
              <path class="accent" d="M690 330 A 70 70 0 0 0 620 275"/>
              <path class="accent" d="M690 330 A 92 92 0 0 0 600 250" stroke-dasharray="6 8"/>
              <circle class="fill-accent" cx="650" cy="318" r="40"/>
              <text class="hand accent-txt" x="560" y="395">thumb sweeps a RADIUS</text>
              <text class="hand small" x="590" y="112">device, held one-handed</text>
              <text class="hand big" x="330" y="70">one hand is ALWAYS full</text>
              <path class="ink-thin" d="M330 80 q 60 10 120 0"/>
              <text class="hand small" x="330" y="105">→ the whole UI belongs to one thumb</text>
            </g>
          </svg>
          <figcaption>From my 2013 whiteboard: the constraint that decided everything. Probe hand on the patient, device hand does it all — and a thumb moves on an arc, not a grid.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ SKETCH 2: RESEARCH ============ -->
      <section class="block container narrow" data-notes="Measured, not guessed. &quot;Hoober, February 2013: 1,333 people in the wild, 75% driving the phone with a thumb, grips 49 / 36 / 15. That's where the thumb zone comes from.&quot; Hold your own phone one-handed: &quot;Easy — the arc your thumb makes for free. Stretch — you can get there but your grip loosens. Ache — you regrip or bring the other hand. For our users a regrip means a hand OFF the patient. So the top of the screen wasn't awkward. It was off limits.&quot; Christoffer: research-literate; Aditya: system thinking.">
        <span class="ghost-word" aria-hidden="true">THUMB</span>
        <h2 class="display-h hollow sec-h">Where a thumb actually lives.</h2>
        <p class="lede" data-reveal>I wasn't guessing. In February 2013 Steven Hoober published a field observation of <strong>1,333</strong> people using their phones in the wild: <strong>75%</strong> drove the interaction with a thumb, and grips split roughly <strong>49% one-handed, 36% cradled, 15% two-handed</strong>. That study is where the "thumb zone" comes from — the sweep radius of a thumb mapped onto the screen: a <em>natural</em> zone at the bottom, a <em>stretch</em> zone in the middle, and an <em>ow</em> zone at the top that needs a regrip or a second hand.</p>
        <p class="lede" data-reveal>Josh Clark's <em>Designing for Touch</em> had laid down the era's gesture rules, and the platforms had published their target sizes — iOS 44&nbsp;pt, Android 48&nbsp;dp, Windows Phone 34&nbsp;px, Nokia a flat 1&nbsp;cm — because a thumb pad is roughly 16–20&nbsp;mm and it doesn't care about your pixel grid. For our users, "regrip" means "hand off the patient." So the top of the screen wasn't merely awkward. It was off limits.</p>
        <div class="cite" data-reveal>
          <p>Hoober, S. (2013, Feb). <a href="https://www.uxmatters.com/mt/archives/2013/02/how-do-users-really-hold-mobile-devices.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>How Do Users Really Hold Mobile Devices?</em></a> UXmatters — 1,333-user field study; 75% thumb; 49/36/15 grip split.</p>
          <p>Clark, J. (2010). <a href="https://abookapart.com/products/designing-for-touch" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>Designing for Touch.</em></a> Thumb-zone ergonomics; gestures need visible affordance.</p>
          <p>Platform touch targets, 2010–12: <a href="https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/layout" target="_blank" rel="noopener">iOS 44×44 pt</a> · <a href="https://developer.android.com/develop/ui/views/touch-and-input/touch-target" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Android 48×48 dp</a> · Windows Phone 34×34 px (26 min) · Nokia 1 cm / 28 px. Finger-pad research (2012): index ≈ 10–14 mm, thumb ≈ 16–20 mm.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="sketch" data-reveal>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 900 460" role="img" aria-label="Hand sketch: device outline with easy, stretch and ache thumb zones, plus three tiny grip sketches labeled 49, 36 and 15 percent.">
            <g filter="url(#sketch)">
              <rect class="ink" x="90" y="40" width="230" height="380" rx="20"/>
              <path class="fill-ache" d="M104 54 h 202 v 76 h -202 z"/>
              <path class="fill-stretch" d="M104 130 h 202 v 150 h -202 z"/>
              <path class="fill-easy" d="M104 280 h 202 v 126 h -202 z"/>
              <text class="hand small onfill" x="120" y="100">OW / ache — top ~20%</text>
              <text class="hand small onfill" x="120" y="210">STRETCH — middle ~40%</text>
              <text class="hand onfill" x="120" y="350">EASY — bottom ~40%</text>
              <text class="hand small onfill" x="120" y="372">the natural sweep</text>
              <path class="accent" d="M300 405 A 150 150 0 0 0 150 280"/>
              <path class="accent" d="M300 405 A 190 190 0 0 0 120 240" stroke-dasharray="6 8"/>
              <text class="hand accent-txt" x="330" y="300">thumb radius</text>
              <text class="hand accent-txt small" x="330" y="322">(~16–20mm pad)</text>
              <text class="hand big" x="470" y="70">how people hold it</text>
              <text class="hand small" x="470" y="92">Hoober, Feb 2013 · n = 1,333</text>
              <rect class="ink-thin" x="480" y="120" width="60" height="100" rx="8"/>
              <path class="ink-thin" d="M540 200 c 20 5, 30 25, 20 45 c -15 20, -55 10, -65 -10"/>
              <text class="hand" x="560" y="150">49%</text><text class="hand small" x="560" y="170">one-handed, thumb</text>
              <rect class="ink-thin" x="480" y="250" width="60" height="100" rx="8"/>
              <path class="ink-thin" d="M470 330 c -10 10, -8 30, 10 40 M550 260 l 20 -20 l 10 10"/>
              <text class="hand" x="560" y="285">36%</text><text class="hand small" x="560" y="305">cradled, other finger</text>
              <rect class="ink-thin" x="480" y="378" width="100" height="62" rx="8"/>
              <path class="ink-thin" d="M470 425 c -8 8, -6 20, 8 26 M590 425 c 8 8, 6 20, -8 26"/>
              <text class="hand" x="600" y="405">15%</text><text class="hand small" x="600" y="425">two thumbs</text>
              <text class="hand" x="640" y="160">75% = THUMB</text>
              <path class="ink-thin" d="M640 168 q 40 8 90 0"/>
              <text class="hand small" x="640" y="200">for our users "regrip"</text>
              <text class="hand small" x="640" y="220">= hand OFF the patient</text>
              <text class="hand accent-txt small" x="640" y="248">→ top of screen: OFF LIMITS</text>
            </g>
          </svg>
          <figcaption>The thumb zone, drawn the way I talked it through at the whiteboard — and why "stretch" was never acceptable for someone whose other hand is holding a probe.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ SKETCH 3: A/B ============ -->
      <section class="block container narrow" data-notes="Show the messy middle. &quot;I started where everyone starts — flyouts, dropdowns, accordions. Fine with two hands. On the iPad Mini, watching people, I saw the hotzones light up: every one of those patterns eventually asks the thumb to reach, and the body answers with a regrip. In this context that's not a usability ding — it's a failure. So the radial motion stopped being 'novel' and became necessary: put the controls on the arc the thumb already travels, and the grip never changes.&quot; Kait: iteration, then ruthless edit.">
        <h2 class="display-h hollow sec-h" data-reveal>The exploration that failed — and the one that didn't.</h2>
        <p class="lede" data-reveal>I started where everyone starts: random-access patterns. Flyout menus. Dropdowns. Accordions. They're fine when you have two hands. On the iPad Mini, watching people actually use them, I saw the hotzones light up immediately — every one of those patterns eventually asked the thumb to reach somewhere it couldn't, and the body answered with a regrip. Regrip means the probe hand moves. That's not a usability ding; in this context it's a failure.</p>
        <p class="lede" data-reveal>So the radial thumb motion stopped being an idea I was exploring because it was novel. It became the only geometry that fit: <strong>put the controls on the arc the thumb already travels, and the grip never changes.</strong></p>
        <figure class="sketch" data-reveal>
