          <p class="eyebrow">Act 2 · first win</p>
          <h2 class="display-h hollow sec-h">Accessibility always enters the picture.</h2>
          <p class="lede">It was never the headline on iViz — the headline was a medic with one hand free. But design the input to fit how the body actually moves, and accessibility arrives as a consequence: a gloved thumb, a gesture that forgives a slip, a control you can operate without looking. That's the discipline, and it's followed me everywhere since.</p>
          <p class="lede">At Microsoft it meant S360 bug bashes — often — and re-designing when the bar said so. I'd prototype, put it in front of the accessibility leads in C+AI, and if I was missing the mark they'd tell me; the dialogue with the people who live these barriers is what sharpened the work. Eventually I was moonlighting for Azure, opening pull requests to fix accessibility bugs myself. Standards are the rigor underneath. <strong>The thesis is who can now do what.</strong></p>
